{"id": "7abc0aa5-4ea5-48b2-92c6-6f16c0a41462", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"id": "tooluse_Fuip65SveIBFsS2VNHNn3p", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-cost-change-analysis", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["storage-ebs-expertise", "investigating-infrastructure-changes", "exploring-metrics"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Explain why the user's Amazon EBS bill jumped this month and identify what changed. Follow the 'Troubleshoot: billing' path of the storage-ebs-expertise skill (read references/troubleshoot-billing.md and references/data-collection.md via read_skill_resource). Read-only only.",
  "context": "User says: 'My EBS bill jumped this month and I don't know why. What changed?' Today is 2026-11-11 (Wednesday), so the current month is partial (Nov 1-11). Compare daily average cost rates, not raw monthly totals, for Nov 1-10 vs prior months (at least Aug-Oct 2026), and pinpoint the day(s) the daily cost stepped up. Agent space account is 012345678901_1 in us-east-1, but use list_associations to find all associated AWS accounts; Cost Explorer data may live only in a payer/management account, and EBS volumes may live in other accounts/regions. EBS charges appear under 'EC2 - Other' in Cost Explorer.",
  "resources": ["Cost Explorer (us-east-1 endpoint)", "EC2 volumes and snapshots in associated accounts/regions", "DLM policies, AWS Backup plans, Recycle Bin rules (if readable)"],
  "timeframe": "2026-08-01T00:00:00Z to 2026-11-11T19:00:00Z",
  "criteria": "Break the change down by EBS usage type (volume storage, provisioned IOPS, provisioned throughput, snapshots) using the account's own Cost Explorer amounts, grouped by USAGE_TYPE and also by REGION/LINKED_ACCOUNT if useful. Then tie each increase to a cause with dates: new volumes (CreateTime), volume modifications (DescribeVolumesModifications: type/size/IOPS/throughput changes such as gp3 to io2 or raised IOPS), unattached 'available' volumes, snapshot growth (DLM / AWS Backup policies, Recycle Bin retention). Optionally use lookup_cloudtrail_events to identify who made key changes; if denied note it as a gap. Do a brief right-sizing check for any volume with large provisioned IOPS/throughput. Report using the troubleshoot verdict format: Answer, Evidence (table of components with both periods' amounts and units), Binding layer (Not applicable), Recommendation (proposals only, with exact parameters and cost direction), Separate findings, Gaps (every API that failed or was denied). Never type list prices. Keep the report concise."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Analyze EBS cost increase and related volume changes"}], "created_at": null}

